  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Manik Dhondiba More vs The State Of Maharashtra And Another on 3 October, 2018 Bench: T.V. Nalawade, Vibha Kankanwadi 1 913-Cri.Appln. 5607-2013.od In The High Court Of Judicature At Bombay Bench At Aurangabad Criminal Application No. 5607 Of 201 3 Manik S/o Dhondiba More, Age : 55 Years, Occ. Business, R/o. Jaikwadi, Vasmat Road, Sonpeth Tq. Sonpeth, Dist. Parbhani. ..Applicant Versus 1] The State of Maharashtra 2] Food Safety Officer, Through Chief Food Safety Officer, Vasamt Road, Parbhani. .. Respondents ... Advocate for the applicants : Mr. G. G. Kadam A.P.P for respondent/ State : Mr. M.M. Nerlikar ... Coram :T.V. Nalawade And Smt.Vibha Kankanwadi,Jj. Date : 03-10-2018 Order :",
    "facts": "On 20.09.2013 at about 5.30 p.m. she inspected Balaji Pan Center of Sonpeth to ascertain as to whether Gutkha which was banned in the State was kept there. In the search, she recovered five packets of Goa gutkha containing 60 pouch each. This gutkha was taken over by her. Manik More was present in the shop. After seizure, she gave report to the Police and crime came to be registered.",
    "arguments": "4] The learned counsel for the applicant mainly submitted that in view of the provisions of aforesaid Special Enactment the crime could not have been registered and the prosecution ought to have been launched by Food Safety Officer by filing complaint before the learned Judicial Magistrate [F.C.]. He also submitted that the provisions of Indian Penal Code mentioned above cannot be used as special enactment is made to take action when food article is involved in the matter. It is laid down by this Manik Dhondiba More vs The State Of Maharashtra And Another on 3 October, 2018 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/164561758/ 2 Court that Guthkha is food and if 4 913-Cri.Appln. 5607-2013.odt it is sold ( as eatable) and the provisions of the Indian Penal Code like section 272 and 328 of the Indian Penal Code are applicable, the crime can be registered.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "4] The learned counsel for the applicant mainly submitted that in view of the provisions of aforesaid Special Enactment the crime could not have been registered and the prosecution ought to have been launched by Food Safety Officer by filing complaint before the learned Judicial Magistrate [F.C.]. He also submitted that the provisions of Indian Penal Code mentioned above cannot be used as special enactment is made to take action when food article is involved in the matter.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It is laid down by this Manik Dhondiba More vs The State Of Maharashtra And Another on 3 October, 2018 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/164561758/ 2 Court that Guthkha is food and if 4 913-Cri.Appln. 5607-2013.odt it is sold ( as eatable) and the provisions of the Indian Penal Code like section 272 and 328 of the Indian Penal Code are applicable, the crime can be registered."
  },
